I'll start getting the most obvious/cited [b]cons[/b] outta the way first:

- It's short. The original wasn't a lengthy game by any stretch, but this feels devoid of key locations from the original and missing previously established plot points/pay-offs (Sorry, Brad);
-It's linear. Well, yeah. Resident Evil 2 Remake (2019) was linear too, but it's backtracking, A/B scenarios, and incorporation of Mr X, kept gameplay fresh, and rewarded multiple replays. 

[b] Pros [/b]

- The action set pieces are some of the best in any Resident Evil game;
- The RE engine brings Raccoon city alive (poor choice of words) with it's photo-realistic graphics, lighting, and sound design;
- Nemesis: to all those saying "he's not in it much", I disagree - he is. I think the shortness of the game doesn't do it justice, but all encounters felt tense, "life threatening" (especially on hardcore), and made me fear him in much the same way as the original;
- The voice acting, whilst limited, is greatly improved. Even if some iconic lines are pushed to the wayside;
- Carlos's improved characterisation - he feels less of a caricature, and they kept, but enhanced, his dynamic with Jill;
- Gameplay is fluid. A continuation of what was shown in RE2R with the return of the RE3 dodge mechanic;
- Soundtrack: it's not hot-swappable like RE2R, but key themes, with new additions, add to the tension of roaming the streets of Raccoon city.

[b] Verdict [/b]

If you're a Resident Evil fan, and enjoyed the RE2R, pick it up. Granted, it's a short campaign, but it's fun, frantic, and if multiplayer is your thing, you've got the inclusion of RE Resistance to boot. 

Otherwise, hold the fort until the next steam sale.

