I hold Resident Evil 3: Nemesis very dearly and it's probably my favorite Resident Evil game of the entire franchise, with that in mind, I gotta say, the Remake is surprisingly good, though it has some pretty jarring flaws and unexpected changes.

The good stuff:
- Combat is a huge improvement from REmake 2, now that Jill and Carlos can do specific timed dodges everything flows better and changes the ''going in circles'' strategy to defeat bosses;
- The remake is absolutely stunning, graphically speaking. The RE Engine truly shines here, especially with the character models and facial animations;
- Jill's personality is way more compelling in this reimagination, sounding and looking more human than ever;
- This new version of the Raccoon City is awesome, though it would have been nicer to have more locations from the original;
- Bunch of cool references to Capcom's games and movies that came out in the 90's
- Extremely well-optimized. Getting rock solid 144fps on a 5700 XT, i7 8700K and 16GB;
- Carlos' segments were pretty good overall, however, his voice acting felt a bit odd at times;
- I prefer this version of the Hunter Gamma to the old and also, the Hunter Beta's mechanic of breaking its parts so that you can do more damage is pretty cool too.

The not so good stuff:
- REmake 3's length can be underwhelming for the majority, even more so if you have never played the original, which was quite short;
- The removal of some fan-favorite areas can be a turn-off for some, which I understand. There were areas that I was really looking forward to seeing, and for some god-forsaken reason, were there but not as I expected (I'm looking at you clock tower); 
- Though the city looks gorgeous and it's great to see some of these locales revamped, exploring feels somewhat limited and backtracking is on the safer side;
- The game feels easier than REmake 2 (took me about 4 hours and 30 minutes to beat it on the Hardcore mode) and it also doesn't have the ink ribbons to save your progress, but, as a sidenote, it has more difficulties beyond Hardcore;
- The gore system has been downgraded. You no longer see limbs getting detached and hanging from zombies' bodies after you shoot them. They just explode, furthermore the zombies don't have ragdoll after they die;


The bad stuff: 
Nemesis. God 'effing dammit, Capcom, you had one job, and you dun goofed. The iconic, unstoppable, stars-hating force of nature, who with his constant overwhelming lurking presence made you sh*t your pants as a kid, now is just a shadow of its former self. Nemesis feels constrained here, never having his moment to really annoy you. Most of the time he appears in game is on a scripted ''OH NO, I GOTTA GET THE F OUTTA HERE'' moment, where you run from point A to point B, and  that really sucks;
- Puzzles are close to non-existent in the remake. Period. I would't call anything they have here a puzzle, especially when comparing to the ludicrously hard puzzles the original one had;
- It feels rushed, not in terms of being broken, buggy or anything like that, but the story and the way it progresses feels way too fast. You already start the game being asked to fix the train...;
- No Mercenaries mode, but we have Resistance (which I haven't played it yet due to the overly long waiting queues);
- No more live selection, so you don't have split paths depending on what you choose;
- Reduced enemy types. The remake has zombies, zombie dogs, lickers, drain deimos and the hunters gamma and beta. No grave diggers, no crows, no spiders. Boo! We do have the pale heads from REmake 2, tho

With all that being said, Resident Evil 3 Remake still is a solid game that took a few wrong turns but still managed to find its way. I recommend it for fans if you're willing to accept that this is not a perfumed, gold-engraved carbon copy of the original, and for people who are interested, but don't really know the franchise, there really isn't a reason for me not to recommend the game aside from its short campaign.

P.S. I will continue to play it, since I still have to beat it on Nightmare and Inferno, get all the unlockables and see if Resistance is any good. 

EDIT: It took me about 20 hours to 100% the game. I still think it's worth it, but maybe wait for a sale if you're not sure.

